1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,761
Where does code go when more than one module needs it?

2
00:00:02,761 --> 00:00:10,261
Security rules and Keycloak calls are needed by every module that takes requests, not only by the partner module.

3
00:00:10,261 --> 00:00:14,488
Copied into each module, every fix would have to be made twice.

4
00:00:14,488 --> 00:00:21,690
So shared code lives in platform libraries, versioned and published, next to the error and OTP libraries.

5
00:00:21,690 --> 00:00:29,511
The security library secures any app: access tokens, realm roles, and refusals as problem details.

6
00:00:29,511 --> 00:00:34,119
Its route rules are configuration, and anything not listed is refused.

7
00:00:34,119 --> 00:00:39,511
It configures itself before Spring Boot's own defaults, and an app can still replace it.

8
00:00:39,511 --> 00:00:46,166
The Keycloak library is a small admin client that logs in as its own client and refreshes its token.

9
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:53,047
The partner module keeps its own identity port, and its Keycloak adapter simply delegates to that client.

10
00:00:53,047 --> 00:00:59,142
A module asks the version catalog for a library by name, and gets one tested, versioned jar.

11
00:00:59,142 --> 00:01:03,059
Next, the money rules begin, with the commission on every payment.

